cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this repository, cite it as below. The raw harvests the
  repository leaves out have their own citation on the Zenodo record
  README.md names (DOI 10.5281/zenodo.23462826).
title: "specdec-sidechannels: three research records on what a speculative-decoding system gives away for free"
version: "v1.0.0"
date-released: "2026-10-19"
repository-code: "https://github.com/Mike-Byrne-AI/specdec-sidechannels"
license: MIT
authors:
  - name: "Michael Byrne"
  - name: "Claude (AI collaborator)"
    email: "claudes.email.address@gmail.com"
keywords:
  - speculative decoding
  - drafter steering
  - side channel
  - pre-registration
  - negative results
  - archive codec
  - arithmetic coding
abstract: >-
  Three research records published as they stood at their pinned commits.
  sideband asked whether the memory-access address stream a weight-serving
  device sees for free can steer a speculative-decoding drafter enough to
  matter; its registered real-model runs were gated by kill criteria written
  in advance and their labels recomputed by an adversarial reviewer from
  committed evidence, the record names its own exceptions, and the headline
  result is negative. amber codes
  archives of model-generated text as a draft model's rank corrections,
  decoded with the draft alone, byte-exact under a measured contract.
  lockstep is a pure-integer arithmetic coder with conformance vectors and a
  proposed model-pinned archive profile that was never built. Every record
  is corrected by dated errata, never rewrites.
